  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how surface charge on polymer insulators causes electrical flashover, how it can be measured, and how it may be reduced. The top section highlights three visible triggering factors: accumulation of positive and negative charges on the insulator surface, surface contamination, and a secondary-electron emission avalanche between cathode and anode. Together these sketches suggest that localized charge build-up and electron multiplication along the polymer surface promote discharge paths.\n\nThe middle section shows characterization methods across different length scales. A dust map gives a coarse view of where charge sits on the insulating material, while an active electrostatic probe appears to scan the sample and produce a surface-charge distribution map with positive and negative regions. Kelvin probe force microscopy provides much finer, nanoscale probing, linked here to an illustration of trapped charges in “deep traps,” implying a microscopic origin for charge accumulation.\n\nThe bottom section presents strategies to raise flashover voltage. These include regulating surface charge through surface or bulk modification, apparently by changing trap energy distributions and conductivity; suppressing electron-avalanche development; making the surface more hydrophobic; and optimizing insulator shape to increase creepage distance. Overall, the figure connects charge physics, measurement, and engineering design.",
